import json
from pathlib import Path
G=[]
def c(title,code,meaning,check,ask,note='',shell='DC Tcl'):
 return dict(title=title,code=code,meaning=meaning,check=check,ask=ask,note=note,shell=shell)
def g(title,scope,intro,cards,sources,steps=None):
 G.append(dict(title=title,scope=scope,intro=intro,cards=cards,sources=sources,steps=steps))
def s(label,url): return dict(label=label,url=url)
vcs=s('Synopsys VCS overview','https://www.synopsys.com/verification/simulation/vcs.html')
dc=s('Synopsys Design Compiler overview','https://www.synopsys.com/implementation-and-signoff/rtl-synthesis-test/design-compiler.html')
pc=s('Synopsys Power Compiler overview','https://www.synopsys.com/implementation-and-signoff/rtl-synthesis-test/power-compiler.html')
g('01 · Server & simulation','Foundations · organizer’s notes','Commands run on the university EDA server after you log in. The setup file belongs to this course; it is not a universal Synopsys installer.',[
c('Load the course environment','tcsh\nsource ~/EE5327.cshrc\nwhich vcs\nwhich dc_shell','Enter tcsh, then load the course’s paths and environment. There is a space after source.','Both which commands resolve to the expected installed tools. A prompt often changes from $ to %, but prompts are configurable.','Why can a tool work in one terminal and be missing in another?',shell='Server terminal'),
c('Build the simulation','vcs -full64 -Mupdate -debug_access+all -kdb fa_st.v fa_gate.v','-full64 selects a 64-bit build. -Mupdate supports incremental builds. -debug_access+all enables broad debug access. -kdb generates the debug database for Verdi integration.','The log shows the intended top/testbench, no fatal errors, and a newly built simv. A successful compile is not a passing test.','What is the difference between parsing RTL, elaborating instances, and executing a simulation?','These switches come from the organizer’s lab notes. Use vcs -help for your installed release. Add -sverilog when compiling SystemVerilog.',shell='Server terminal'),
c('Run and inspect','./simv\n# Or start the configured debug GUI:\n./simv -gui','simv executes the compiled simulation. The GUI option opens the configured debugger; the course uses Verdi.','Advance simulation time, add relevant signals, and verify clock/reset plus the failing transaction. Inspect the testbench PASS/FAIL result.','If the waveform is flat, how do you distinguish missing stimulus, stopped simulation, and missing signal capture?',shell='Server terminal')],[vcs,'Organizer-provided server / VCS command notes; Labs 1–2 were not attached.'],['Compile','Elaborate','Build simv','Run','Debug'])
g('02 · Libraries & setup','Lab 3','DC stands for Design Compiler. Synthesis maps your RTL to technology cells; it does not finish placement, routing, or physical signoff.',[
c('Tell DC where and what to use','set_app_var search_path [concat $search_path ./rtl ./libs]\nset_app_var target_library "N16ADFP_StdCelltt0p8v25c_ccs.db"\nset_app_var link_library "* N16ADFP_StdCelltt0p8v25c_ccs.db dw_foundation.sldb"\ndefine_design_lib WORK -path ./work','search_path locates files. target_library provides mapping cells. link_library resolves referenced designs/cells; * includes designs already in memory. WORK stores analyzed HDL data.','Use the exact .synopsys_dc.setup spelling. Check library loading messages and unresolved references after link.','Why can a library be in link_library but not target_library?','The N16 name is from the course. Library files are not included. DesignWare availability depends on installation/licensing.'),
c('Read a library filename','','.db is Synopsys’s compiled library format. In this course name, tt indicates typical NMOS/PMOS, 0p8v means 0.8 V, 25c means 25 °C, and ccs denotes Composite Current Source modeling.','Inspect the library’s actual units, cells, and characterization. A filename alone is not signoff evidence.','What information is in a timing library that is not in LEF—and vice versa?','Liberty/.db describes functions and electrical models. LEF describes physical abstracts such as geometry and pin locations; neither substitutes for a foundry rule deck.')],['Uploaded Lab 3: library setup and synthesis reference flow',dc])
g('03 · Read, link, synthesize','Lab 3','Keep structural checks and timing checks visible. A tool completing without errors does not prove the intended design was constrained.',[
c('Construct the design','analyze -format verilog {fulladd_gate.v add4_struct.v}\nelaborate add4_struct\ncurrent_design add4_struct\nlink\ncheck_design','Analyze reads HDL; elaborate resolves the top, parameters, and hierarchy. current_design makes the target explicit. link resolves references; check_design catches structural issues.','No unresolved modules, unexpected black boxes, unintended latches, multiple drivers, or undriven essential signals.','What can elaborate catch that a syntax-only check cannot?','Use the module names actually present in your files. Some flows link implicitly; keep explicit link and inspect its diagnostics.'),
c('Map, inspect, and save','compile\nreport_area\nreport_timing\nreport_constraint -all_violators\ncheck_timing\nwrite_file -format ddc -hierarchy -output mapped.ddc\nwrite_file -format verilog -hierarchy -output mapped.v\nwrite_sdc mapped.sdc\nstart_gui','Compile maps and optimizes. Reports expose the result. Save the tool database, mapped netlist, and constraints for reproducibility.','Timing is constrained; area is plausible; the chosen libraries and ports are correct. Review the worst path, not only the headline slack.','Why might positive synthesis slack become negative after routing?','A .ddc is a DC database; a .v netlist and .sdc constraints have different roles. start_gui opens DC’s GUI, not a waveform simulator.')],['Uploaded Lab 3, synthesis flow and deliverables',dc])
g('04 · Timing budgets','Lab 3 · SDC basics','Input and output delays model the surrounding system. They do not insert gates or consume a percentage of the clock automatically.',[
c('Constrain a combinational block','create_clock -name CLK -period 1\nset_input_delay 0.08 -clock [get_clocks CLK] [all_inputs]\nset_output_delay 0.12 -clock [get_clocks CLK] [all_outputs]','With no source object, CLK is virtual. For this simplified max-delay example, input arrives at 0.08 and output must arrive by 0.88: internal budget is 0.80 time units.','Verify library time units (ns in the lab). At 0.60 output arrival, slack is 0.88 - 0.60 = +0.28 ns.','If 0.60 is internal delay rather than arrival time, what changes?','For production I/O signoff specify -min and -max from external timing requirements. This simplified lab example assumes no other latency or uncertainty.'),
c('Use a real clock on a sequential design','create_clock -name CLK -period 2 [get_ports clk]\nset data_inputs [remove_from_collection [all_inputs] [get_ports {clk rst_n}]]\nset_input_delay -max 0.20 -clock CLK $data_inputs\nset_input_delay -min 0.05 -clock CLK $data_inputs\nset_output_delay -max 0.25 -clock CLK [all_outputs]\nset_output_delay -min -0.05 -clock CLK [all_outputs]','Attach a clock to the actual port. Constrain data ports separately from the clock and reset. Negative output min delay can model positive external hold time in a simple zero-skew interface.','These example numbers must be replaced with interface requirements. Analyze reset recovery/removal and any exceptions deliberately.','Why is applying data input delay to the clock port a mistake?','Do not copy the example into an unknown design. Reset policy, clock uncertainty, generated clocks, and clock-domain relationships require separate constraints.'),
c('Model the environment electrically','set_driving_cell -lib_cell BUFFD2BWP20P90 \\\n  -input_transition_rise 0.1 -input_transition_fall 0.1 [all_inputs]\nset_load [expr {[load_of [get_lib_pins */BUFFD2BWP20P90/I]] * 4}] [all_outputs]','Driving-cell modeling estimates input slew from an external cell. Output load models capacitance, here four reference input-pin loads. These are separate from I/O timing delays.','Ensure the library pin selection is unambiguous, units match, and load is realistic. Adapt port selection for sequential designs.','Can a circuit meet timing with an unrealistic output load? What would that prove?')],['Uploaded Lab 3: virtual clock, I/O delays, driving cell and load sections'])
g('05 · Read reports','Labs 3–4 · beyond','Always identify units, corner, mode, path group, and analysis stage before drawing a conclusion.',[
c('Ask both timing questions','report_timing -delay_type max\nreport_timing -delay_type min\nreport_constraint -all_violators\ncheck_timing','Max-delay analysis examines setup-style requirements; min-delay analysis examines hold-style requirements. check_timing identifies missing or inconsistent timing setup.','Setup slack = required - arrival. Hold slack = arrival - required. Read startpoint, endpoint, clocks, cell delay, and net delay.','Why can lowering the clock frequency fix setup but usually not a same-edge hold failure?'),
c('Compare methods fairly','','Record RTL revision, library/corner, SDC, compile recipe, area, WNS/TNS, violating endpoints, and power activity. Change one factor at a time.','Confirm runs constrain the same paths and use the same timing/power assumptions before ranking them.','Would you choose lower area if it misses timing? How would you present the Pareto tradeoff?'),
c('Lab 4 optimization directives','set_structure true -boolean true -timing true\ncompile -exact_map -boundary_optimization','This is one Lab 4 recipe for timing-driven structuring with Boolean optimization. Compare against the handout’s flattening experiments in separate clean runs.','Compare the mapped schematic and reports. Preserve a baseline; do not mix stale constraints or directives across experiments.','How can logic sharing save area but create a high-fanout timing bottleneck?','Legacy commands/options depend on release. The handout’s set_flatten is logic minimization; do not confuse it with simply removing HDL hierarchy.')],['Uploaded Lab 4: decoder optimization experiments','Uploaded Lab 3: setup and hold reporting'])
g('06 · FSM experiments','Lab 5 · updated release notes','The updated lab compares explicitly pre-encoded binary and one-hot RTL. Old extracted-FSM commands shown in background sections are not a reliable current recipe.',[
c('Use the supported lab path','set_app_var fsm_auto_inferring true\nset_app_var fsm_enable_state_minimization true\n# Read one pre-encoded RTL variant in a clean run.\ncompile_ultra\nreport_fsm\nreport_area\nreport_timing','Run the binary and one-hot variants separately with identical constraints, then inspect whether encoding was preserved and whether the FSM was recognized.','The updated lab expects Re-Encoding style: NONE. Record state registers, area, and worst reg-to-reg slack.','Why might one-hot reduce decode depth but increase clock power?','Lab 5 documents CMD-080 for set_fsm_encoding_style in syn_2022.12. Do not paste the older background FSM command chain.'),
c('Choose based on evidence','','Binary uses ceil(log2(N)) state bits; one-hot typically uses N. Gray-like coding can reduce switching for selected transitions, but arbitrary FSM transitions are not all one-bit changes.','Check illegal-state behavior, reset, output timing, and equivalent externally visible behavior for every encoding.','Would a safety requirement change how you handle unused encodings?')],['Uploaded Lab 5: updated binary/one-hot RTL procedure and final questions'])
g('07 · Scan & ATPG','Lab 6 · legacy course flow','Functional verification checks behavior. Manufacturing test seeks modeled physical faults. TetraMAX is the course tool; newer flows and licenses may use different products.',[
c('Make test intent explicit','set_scan_configuration -style multiplexed_flip_flop\n# Declare actual clock/reset/test ports with set_dft_signal.\ncreate_test_protocol\ndft_drc -verbose\ncompile_ultra -scan','Configure the scan style, declare test intent, build a protocol, and run pre-DFT checks. Test-ready synthesis can replace functional flops with scan-capable cells.','Verify controllable clocks/reset and inspect unscannable registers. Scan replacement is not the same as scan-chain stitching.','Why can one uncontrollable reset make ATPG ineffective?','This is a flow fragment. Supply your real scan-clock timing, active reset polarity, ports, and license-supported options.'),
c('Stitch and recheck','set_dft_signal -view spec -type ScanDataIn -port test_si\nset_dft_signal -view spec -type ScanDataOut -port test_so\nset_dft_signal -view spec -type ScanEnable -port test_se\ncreate_test_protocol -infer_asynch -infer_clock\ndft_drc -verbose\nset_scan_configuration -replace false\ninsert_dft\ndft_drc -verbose -coverage\nreport_constraint -all_violators','With scan-replaced flops already present, stitch the chain and recheck test rules plus functional constraints.','Save matching pre/post-DFT netlists and protocols. Recheck functional equivalence, area, and timing after insertion.','What is the area and test-time tradeoff between one long chain and several shorter chains?','The -replace false setting assumes the earlier test-ready compile already replaced eligible flops.'),
c('Generate and analyze patterns','tmax -gui\n# In TetraMAX, load the library models and matching netlist.\n# Build the model, read the matching STIL protocol, and pass DRC.\n# Select the fault model, seed faults, run ATPG, then inspect classes.','Separate build, test DRC, and ATPG. Compare basic scan, fast sequential, and full sequential with controlled settings.','Check DT, UD, AU, ND and the total. Fault coverage and test coverage use different denominators.','Can 100% test coverage coexist with less than 100% fault coverage?','Lab 6 mixes stuck-at instructions with transition-fault sample reports. Record the actual chosen fault model; do not compare unlike fault models.',shell='Server terminal / TetraMAX workflow')],['Uploaded Lab 6: DFT insertion, TetraMAX procedure and sample coverage reports'])
g('08 · Activity & power','Lab 7','Dynamic power depends on activity, capacitance, voltage, and frequency. Leakage is a different component. A smaller reported number is meaningful only under comparable conditions.',[
c('Describe switching honestly','set_switching_activity -static_probability 0.01 \\\n  -toggle_rate 1.5625 -period 10 [get_ports enable]\nset_switching_activity -static_probability 0.5 \\\n  -toggle_rate 15.625 -period 10 [get_ports up]\nset_switching_activity -static_probability 0.995 \\\n  -toggle_rate 0.015625 -period 10 [get_ports rst]','The updated lab’s table gives P(0) and toggles per 10 ns. -static_probability is P(1), so convert using 1 - P(0).','Verify design time units are ns and port names match. Inspect annotation coverage rather than assuming the activity reached every node.','Does 1.5625 toggles per interval imply a 1.5625 Hz frequency? Why not?','If using a SAIF workflow beyond the lab, validate instance mapping and simulation window; reset-heavy or idle-only traces can mislead.'),
c('Read the power evidence','report_power -analysis_effort high -verbose\nreport_saif -hier\nreport_threshold_voltage_group','Break out internal, net switching, and leakage power. Check activity annotation and the actual cell-threshold mix.','Compare area, timing, and power using the same corner, activity, clock period, and units.','If power drops but timing fails, is that an optimization success?','The handout’s extreme clock-period experiment is optimization pressure, not proof that the circuit operates at 1 THz.')],['Uploaded Lab 7, pp. 7–10 and 15–17',pc])
g('09 · Clock gating & Vt','Lab 7','Clock gating targets clock-driven switching; multi-Vt selection trades delay against leakage. Neither is free.',[
c('Infer integrated clock gates','set_clock_gating_style -pos integrated -control_point before\ncompile_ultra -gate_clock\nreport_clock_gating\nreport_power -analysis_effort high -verbose\nreport_timing','Use the corrected positive-edge integrated-gating style in the handout and synthesize eligible enable-controlled registers with clock gating.','Confirm gates were inserted and which registers are covered. Check enable timing, test enable, area overhead, and post-CTS consequences.','Why is assign gclk = clk & enable unsafe if enable can change while clk is high?','Exact ICG availability depends on the library. Clock-gating checks and test behavior still matter after insertion.'),
c('Explore threshold choices','man set_multi_vth_constraint\nreport_threshold_voltage_group','Use your release’s documented constraint to bound low-Vt usage; inspect whether the limit applies by count or area and whether it is hard or soft.','Measure the achieved mix and slack. Low-Vt often speeds cells but increases leakage; high/standard-Vt can save leakage on paths with margin.','Why is “replace every cell with low-Vt” a poor default?','Do not guess percentage/group option names across releases. The lab requests a 10% LVT-control experiment; reproduce the exact documented local syntax.')],['Uploaded Lab 7: corrected gating style, Vt experiments and questions',pc])
g('10 · Cadence counterpart','Beyond Lab 7 · vendor workflows','Learn the operation, then its tool-specific command. Cadence Common UI and legacy UI differ; do not blindly translate a DC script.',[
c('Simulate with Xcelium','xrun -64bit -sv -gui dut.sv tb.sv','A typical Xcelium invocation compiles, elaborates, and simulates SystemVerilog, with GUI debug.','Use your local xrun help and record compile order, top module, seed, and debug-access settings.','Which parts of your testbench should stay portable between VCS and Xcelium?',shell='Server terminal'),
c('Synthesize with Genus','','Read timing libraries and HDL → elaborate top → read SDC → generic synthesis → technology mapping → optimization → reports and exports.','Inspect resolved hierarchy, constraints, cell mapping, timing, and area. Use the installed Genus reference flow for exact commands.','Does a common SDC file guarantee identical results in DC and Genus?','Genus uses its own library and database setup. The conceptual flow is shown here rather than an unverified, release-specific full script.'),
c('Implement with Innovus','','Import netlist, technology/cell abstracts, timing views and SDC → floorplan/power plan → placement → CTS → routing → extraction/signoff feedback.','At each checkpoint inspect timing, congestion, utilization, power connectivity, and design-rule violations.','Why is a clean pre-CTS setup report insufficient for signoff?','Use Innovus/Tempus/Quantus/Voltus for related implementation, timing, extraction, and rail-analysis tasks. They are separate analyses, even when integrated.')],[s('Cadence Xcelium GUI command discussion','https://community.cadence.com/cadence_technology_forums/f/logic-design/47187/what-is-the-command-for-opening-the-xcelium-simulator'),s('Cadence digital flow and tools','https://www.cadence.com/en_US/home/explore/what-is-ic-design.html'),s('Cadence Innovus datasheet','https://www.cadence.com/en_US/home/resources/datasheets/innovus-implementation-system-ds.html')])
g('11 · Physical design methods','Beyond Lab 7 · digital','Fix the cause exposed by the physical evidence. Do not apply the same optimization to every negative-slack path.',[
c('Cell delay or wire delay?','','Cell dominated: consider sizing, Vt, logic restructuring, or pipelining when architecture allows. Wire dominated: investigate placement, fanout, buffering, layer choice, and routing detours.','Compare the delay breakdown before/after; recheck power, hold, congestion, and all relevant corners.','Why might upsizing every gate make routing worse?'),
c('Floorplan and clock choices','','Explore utilization, macro orientation, channels, halos, pin placement, and power-grid access. After CTS, evaluate clock latency, skew, transition, and endpoint timing.','A floorplan with low average utilization can still have a congested macro channel. Inspect local demand.','When would you change the floorplan instead of accepting another timing ECO?'),
c('Close across modes and corners','','Check functional/test modes, min/max timing, RC and PVT corners, crosstalk, variation, EM/IR, and physical rules. Re-extract after routing changes.','Use a corner-by-corner comparison and record new violations; an ECO that improves one path can damage another.','How can useful skew help setup but hurt hold?')],[s('Synopsys ICC II training: block implementation topics','https://training.synopsys.com/learn/courses/997/ic-compiler-ii-block-level-implementation'),s('Synopsys PrimeTime capabilities','https://www.synopsys.com/implementation-and-signoff/signoff/primetime.html'),s('Cadence Tempus datasheet','https://www.cadence.com/en_US/home/resources/datasheets/tempus-timing-signoff-solution-ds.html'),s('Synopsys RedHawk-SC power integrity','https://www.synopsys.com/implementation-and-signoff/signoff/redhawk-sc.html?campaignID=7013g000000YT39AAG')])
g('12 · Analog layout loop','Beyond Lab 7 · analog / custom PD','Analog physical design preserves electrical intent at device and interconnect level. There is no universal layout recipe independent of the circuit and PDK.',[
c('Capture intent before placement','','Identify matched groups, ratios, sensitive nodes, symmetry requirements, current paths, isolation, and relevant device orientation constraints.','Use unit devices where appropriate; compare centroid positions, environment, routing, contacts, and well proximity.','When might common-centroid placement be less useful than a compact, carefully routed pair?'),
c('DRC, LVS, extraction, simulation','','DRC checks geometry against rules. LVS checks extracted connectivity/devices against the source. PEX extracts parasitics. Post-layout simulation checks performance with those parasitics.','A DRC/LVS-clean block can still fail bandwidth, offset, noise, stability, or reliability requirements.','What would you inspect if gain barely changes after layout but phase margin collapses?'),
c('Use corners and mismatch analysis','','Run appropriate process/voltage/temperature corners and statistical mismatch analysis with foundry-supported models. Compare schematic and extracted results using matched conditions.','A corner sweep and Monte Carlo answer different questions. Track yield assumptions and sample size rather than claiming certainty from a handful of runs.','Can a common-centroid layout eliminate random mismatch?','Cadence: Virtuoso, Spectre, Quantus, Pegasus/PVS. Synopsys: Custom Compiler, PrimeSim, StarRC, IC Validator. Follow the licensed PDK-qualified flow.')],[s('Cadence Virtuoso Layout Suite','https://www.cadence.com/en_US/home/tools/custom-ic-analog-rf-design/layout-design/virtuoso-layout-suite.html'),s('Cadence Virtuoso layout capabilities','https://www.cadence.com/en_US/home/resources/datasheets/virtuoso-layout-suite-ds.html'),s('Synopsys analog design platform','https://www.synopsys.com/glossary/what-is-analog-design.html')],['Intent','Layout','DRC / LVS','PEX','Post-layout simulation'])
Path('/workspace/sites/sasi-asic-practice/dist/guide.json').write_text(json.dumps(G,indent=2))
print(len(G),'guide topics;',sum(len(g['cards']) for g in G),'cards')
